WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
A few months ago, I got a message
from someone in Slovenia,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.721
telling me about the Cicka,

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:07.131
a very strange and
very sketchy-looking

00:00:07.131 --> 00:00:09.900
manual cable car across
the river Sava.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.300
And I immediately wanted to
visit and film it.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.400
The problem is,
it's badly documented,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.720
and what there is,
isn't in English.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.840
So I hired a local production team,
who very quickly told me:

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.280
it's been closed.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.460
Like most of the manual cable cars
in this part of the world,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.980
it wasn't being maintained,
and it was becoming unsafe.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.160
Fixing it up to modern standards
or replacing it

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.640
would be far too expensive
just so a few hikers can use it.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.820
But one of the perks of
having local producers

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.700
is that they can find things
that I really can't.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.760
Because not only did they
find a different cable car,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.520
one of the last still-working ones,
with a completely different

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.900
and even sketchier design...

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.260
they found the man who still
maintains it to this day.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.260
[IN SLOVENIAN:]
 
 

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.140
Safety here,
like the sign says,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.700
is on your own
responsibility.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.880
This type of cable car goes back
to the first world war.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.740
The mountains here were part
of the frontline,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.280
and cable cars like this were
installed as makeshift bridges.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.900
But this one's from the 1950s,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.300
built after an old bridge
put up by the Italian army

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.980
got washed out by a flood.

00:02:53.632 --> 00:02:57.540
And I know, this looks sketchy.
It sounds sketchy!

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.840
It squeaks, it bounces,
it sways.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.740
But now I'm standing here,
it feels solid.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.500
I have no worries about this.
Except:

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.740
if I draw attention here, could the
government suddenly decide

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.140
"well, actually, this is against
regulations" and step in?

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.180
Or could some local kids
decide to visit,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.360
and play around and break it, or
vandalise it? Or maybe get hurt?

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.940
I've got a duty of care
to the places I visit.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.540
And this is something that the locals
are clearly very proud of.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.460
A couple of them used it while
we've been filming!

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.160
Most of the manual cable cars
are gone now,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.420
and as far as I can tell,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.580
there's no definitive list
of those that remain.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.440
One day, they'll be history...

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.320
but then, one day,
so will everything.

